{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And I charged you at that time, etc.</b> Rashi explained: these are the ten things that distinguish civil laws from capital cases, and they are in the Sifre — the Re'em cited them — and the gist of them is that civil cases are concluded on that very day, whether for acquittal or for conviction, while capital cases are concluded on that day for acquittal and on the following day for conviction. And it would appear that it was concerning this that he said: 'וָאֲצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם בָּעֵת הַהִיא אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר תַּעֲשׂוּן' — and this verse is inverted (מְסֹרָס), as though he said: 'And I charged you all the things that you shall do at that time' — 'or on the following day.' And concerning this the prophet rebuked them, saying (Isaiah 1:21): 'מְלֵאֲתִי מִשְׁפָּט צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ וְעַתָּה מְרַצְּחִים' — for in capital cases one stays overnight (מַלִּינִין) when the verdict is for conviction, and concludes immediately when it is for acquittal, but they reversed the procedure: 'צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ' — if it appeared to them that he was righteous in his judgment, then 'he stays overnight' — they would delay the acquittal, perhaps they would find grounds for conviction; 'וְעַתָּה מְרַצְּחִים' — if it appeared in their eyes on that very day to be for conviction, then 'and now' immediately, without overnight stay, they were ready to murder him and conclude his judgment for conviction." "But it is still difficult for me according to this explanation: why did he say 'וָאֲצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם' — he should have said 'וָאֲצַוֶּה אֶת שׁוֹפְטֵיכֶם' ('And I charged your judges')? And it would appear that this can be resolved along the lines of what we find (Sanhedrin 8a) — that Rav Naḥman bar Yitzḥak derived from what is stated: 'וְהַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר יִקְשֶׁה מִכֶּם תַּקְרִיבוּן אֵלַי וּשְׁמַעְתִּיו' — that is, 'and I will hear it' from the mouth of the Almighty. And this is sound, for how could Moses, the humblest of men, presume upon himself? Yet Moses still did not mention that after he would hear from the mouth of the Almighty he would return to them the word of HaShem. And it would appear that concerning this he said: 'וָאֲצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם בָּעֵת הַהִיא אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר תַּעֲשׂוּן' — and this verse is connected to what precedes it (מְחֻבָּר אֶשְׁלְמַעְלָה): 'The matter that is too difficult for you, you shall bring to me' — and I will enter before the Almighty 'and I will hear it,' and after I hear from the mouth of the Almighty — 'וָאֲצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם,' meaning: I will give you an answer immediately, without delay; rather, 'בָּעֵת הַהִיא' — at the time that you ask of me, I will answer you what you shall do. And do not think: who knows whether at such a time I will be able to reach the King, King of kings, to ask Him a matter of law — and perhaps through this they would come to oppression of justice; rather, immediately 'בָּעֵת הַהִיא' I will answer the one who asks me a matter, for thus am I assured, as it is stated (Numbers 9:8): 'עִמְדוּ וְאֶשְׁמְעָה מַה יְצַוֶּה ה׳ לָכֶם' — Rashi explained: happy is one born of woman who was so assured that whenever he wished he would speak with the Shekhinah; therefore it says 'עִמְדוּ וְאֶשְׁמְעָה' — do not go from here, rather 'stand here' and immediately 'I will hear what HaShem will command.' And concerning this he said 'וָאֲצַוֶּה אֶתְכֶם בָּעֵת הַהִיא' etc. — and this is a precious interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:14.499607086Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}